==========================================
DAY 14 NOTES — Environment Variables & Shell Config
Date: 11-09-2026
==========================================

WHAT I LEARNED TODAY:
- Environment variables are named values the shell/programs check ($HOME, $USER, $PATH)
- PATH is a colon-separated list of folders bash searches to find commands
- export VAR="value" sets a variable, but only for the CURRENT terminal session
- ~/.bashrc runs every time a new terminal opens — this is where PERMANENT settings go
- source ~/.bashrc reloads config without needing to close/reopen the terminal
- Forgetting "export" means child processes/scripts can't see the variable
- Aliases (like ll='ls -la') are shortcuts, also made permanent via .bashrc

COMMANDS I PRACTICED:
[ ] echo $HOME / $USER / $PATH
[ ] export (temporary variable)
[ ] editing ~/.bashrc
[ ] source ~/.bashrc
[ ] adding an alias

fill this in after running commands yourself :
$ echo $PATH
> 

$ echo $PATH | tr ':' '\n' | wc -l
> 

$ source ~/.bashrc (after adding a variable)
> 


